{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["And the reason for <b>“When your son asks you tomorrow, saying: What are the testimonies”</b> is that he asks first about what these commandments that are called <b>“testimonies”</b> testify to, because they are a remembrance of His wonders and a testimony through them, such as matzah, the sukkah, the pesach, the Sabbath, tefillin, and the mezuzah. And <b>“the statutes”</b> — those whose reason is hidden in the Torah. And <b>“the judgments”</b> — he asks what the judgments are that we execute for these commandments: that we stone the one who performs labor on the Sabbath, and we burn the one who lies with a woman and her mother, and we administer the forty lashes to the one who sows mixed seed, for the judgments of civic governance — the laws of the ox, the pit, the bailees, and the rest of the laws in the Torah — are righteous and good; all who see them acknowledge them.\n\nAnd He commanded that in response to this question we present to the questioner the entire matter of the exodus from Egypt, and the intention in this is the reason behind what is stated in the Ten Commandments (Deuteronomy 5:6), “who brought you out of the land of Egypt” — for the intention is that we make known to the questioning son that YHWH is the Creator, the One who desires, and the Omnipotent, as was explained to us through the exodus from Egypt.\n\nAnd this is the meaning of <b>“before our eyes”</b> — for we are the ones who know and are witnesses, from the signs and wonders that we saw there, that HaShem our Elohim is Elohim in the heavens and on earth and there is none besides Him, for all of this would become known through the exodus from Egypt, as I explained in the first commandment (Exodus 20:2).\n\nAnd behold, it is fitting for us to give honor to His name, for He is our Creator, and He who magnified His kindness with us, and He commanded us to perform these statutes that are mentioned in “testimonies, statutes, and judgments” — to fear Him through our performance of the testimonies as a remembrance of His wonders; for our good through the performance of the statutes, for they are good, there is no statute among them that contains any harm at all, even though its reason is not clarified to all; <b>“to keep us alive, as at this day”</b> — through the judgments, for through them we live, and all of them are good, there is no harm in them, but rather they all bring about good life in the end.\n\nAnd behold, we are obligated to do the will of the Creator, who is our Elohim, and we are His people and the flock of His hand, and there is in all His commandments nothing but good, until it becomes for us righteousness before YHWH our Elohim, and He gives us good reward for the performance of all these commandments. He calls the reward for commandments “righteousness,” for a slave acquired by his master, who is obligated to serve him — if he additionally gives him reward for his service, he performs righteousness with him."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:04.074850813Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}